================================================================================
PATH B production hardening: broad rank-0 I/O guard (shared run directory)
================================================================================
Date     : 2026-06-10
Branch   : mpi-parallel
Binary   : GPU2/run_versions/nbody7b.mpi-cpu  (pure-MPI, OMP_NUM_THREADS=1)
Harness  : mpi_design/poc_validation/run_io_guard.sh   (new, shared-dir np4)
           mpi_design/poc_validation/run_equiv.sh      (per-rank-dir, guard off)
Input    : input_short (13049-body BSE run to END RUN,
           standalone_version/test_cpu_with_bse ICs)

WHAT CHANGED  (closes the "broad-I/O deferred item" of commit 586cd98)
  Before: every rank replicated every WRITE, so a multi-rank run REQUIRED the
  per-rank-directory wrapper (run_equiv.sh wrap.sh) to avoid fort.* write
  races. Now `mpirun -np N` is safe in ONE shared working directory: with the
  guard at its production default (np > 1, NBODY_RANK0_IO unset) only rank 0
  writes the output files and stdout.

  Mechanism (validated empirically with the Amuse-env gfortran: 85+
  simultaneous unit connections to /dev/null, unit-6 re-OPEN, CLOSE +
  re-OPEN with a different FORM):
  - Ncode/mpi_nbody.h      : + /MPIIOG/ RANK0_IO guard flag.
  - GPU2/mpi_nbody.f       : NBODY_MPI_INIT reads NBODY_RANK0_IO (default ON
    for np>1) and on ranks > 0 calls NBODY_MPI_IO_GUARD, which connects unit 6
    and the 83 write-only units of the link set (81 formatted + 82/83
    unformatted) to /dev/null. Units that are READ during the run stay live on
    every rank: 5 (stdin incl. lazy mid-run ksint/chain reads), 10 (fort.10
    ICs), 12 (instar), 222 (input_bse), 1/2 (restart reads).
    + NBODY_IORANK() (does this rank do file output), NBODY_NULL_OPEN
    (re-connect a unit to /dev/null with the right FORM), NBODY_STOP_PROBE
    (rank 0 probes the STOP file, MPI_BCAST of the result -- independent
    probes of a shared STOP file could split across ranks and deadlock the
    next collective), NBODY_BCAST_R8 (rank 0's CPU clock decides the
    TCOMP < CPU termination branch for all ranks -- per-rank CPU times drift,
    a split decision would deadlock).
  - GPU2/mpi_nbody_stub.f  : serial counterparts (NBODY_IORANK always .TRUE.,
    NBODY_STOP_PROBE = the original local probe, BCAST no-op) -> serial and
    AMUSE builds keep the exact original behaviour.
  - GPU2/intgrt.omp.f      : STOP-file probe + CPU-limit check go through the
    two new collectives-safe helpers (timer path, every NMAX steps -> cheap).
  - Block/mydump.f         : save path (II != 0) returns early on non-I/O
    ranks; covers ALL dump call sites (ADJUST fort.2 + fort.1, INTGRT timer,
    end-of-run). Read path (restart) unchanged on every rank. The bespoke
    MYRANK guard added to GPU2/adjust.f by 586cd98 is superseded and removed.
  - 17 explicit OPEN(FILE=...) output sites (OUT3/OUT9/OUT33/OUT34/CMPACT/
    ESC/COLL/COAL/COAL2/MDOT/BINEV/HIARCH/HIDAT/DEGEN/NS/BH/ROCHE + hrplot
    fort.82/83) in 14 Block files: ranks > 0 connect the unit to /dev/null
    via NBODY_NULL_OPEN instead of opening the shared file (STATUS='NEW'
    would also crash on a shared existing file).
  - GPU2/scale.f, Block/setup.f : the KZ(22)=1 IC write to unit 10 is rank-0
    only (fort.10 is the shared IC INPUT file of every rank).
  - GPU2/irrlib/gpuirr.cpp : NBSTAT (neighbour-histogram diagnostic written by
    gpuirr_close via C fopen, out of reach of the Fortran guard) is skipped on
    ranks > 0, detected from the launcher env (OMPI/PMIX/PMI/SLURM rank vars),
    honouring the same NBODY_RANK0_IO=0 opt-out.

VALIDATION (M4 Pro, Amuse-env, OMP_NUM_THREADS=1)

1. run_io_guard.sh -- np4, ONE shared directory, guard ON (production default),
   vs np1 reference in its own directory:                              PASS
   - mpirun exit 0, END RUN reached, guard banner present;
   - ADJUST / END RUN physics lines bit-identical to np1 (WTIME stripped);
   - END RUN appears exactly ONCE in run.out (ranks > 0 fully silenced);
   - file inventory of the shared dir IDENTICAL to the np1 run (20 files,
     no stray rank>0 fort.*);
   - every output file byte-identical to np1: OUT3, OUT33, CMPACT, fort.14,
     fort.31, fort.32, fort.52, fort.56, fort.82, fort.83, fort.821,
     fort.831, fort.99, fort.991, fort.992;
   - fort.1 / fort.2 (COMMON dumps): same size (11106656 B), 10 differing
     bytes = the CPU/wall-time accumulators of COMMON/COUNTS/ in record 1
     (differ between ANY two runs, serial included);
   - NBSTAT present once (per-process diagnostic; at np>1 its histogram
     covers rank 0's irregular-force slice only -- documented behaviour).

2. run_equiv.sh (now exports NBODY_RANK0_IO=0 in wrap.sh) -- per-rank dirs,
   guard OFF, np 1/2/4:                                                PASS
   bit-identical ADJUST + END RUN lines across np1/np2(2 ranks)/np4(4 ranks),
   i.e. the legacy validation mode is preserved and physics is untouched.

3. Serial / AMUSE regression: `make cpu-objects` (the AMUSE worker's compile
   path, links mpi_nbody_stub.o) builds clean; stub defines nbody_iorank_,
   nbody_null_open_, nbody_stop_probe_, nbody_bcast_r8_ so all shared call
   sites resolve. RANK0_IO is statically .FALSE. in serial builds -> original
   I/O instruction stream. (The `make cpu` THROWAWAY LINK fails on macOS with
   the pre-existing -lstdc++ SDK quirk noted in 01_data_distribution.md §9 --
   unrelated to this change; the AMUSE build avoids it via cpu-objects.)

PRODUCTION USAGE NOTES
  - Single shared directory:  mpirun -np 8 bash -c 'exec ./nbody7b.mpi-cpu \
    < input'  (the exec-with-redirect gives EVERY rank the input on stdin;
    plain `mpirun ... < input` forwards stdin to rank 0 only, and NBODY7
    performs lazy mid-run unit-5 reads on every rank. `mpirun --stdin all`
    is the alternative where supported.)
  - STOP-file termination now works correctly in a shared directory (rank-0
    probe + broadcast); with per-rank dirs (guard off) touch STOP in rank 0's
    dir -- it now governs all ranks.
  - NBODY_RANK0_IO=0 restores the old every-rank-writes behaviour (use with
    per-rank directories, e.g. for cross-rank bit-identity validation).
  - Restart dumps fort.1/fort.2 are written by rank 0 only (canonical,
    replicated state); on restart every rank reads the same fort.1.
